{"v":1,"accession":"PRJEB114669","title":"Biofilm samples from a rotating biological contactor treating real human urine across various dilutions","description":"Decentral, source-separated urine treatment with partial nitritation/anammox (PN/A) provides a resource-efficient way to remove nitrogen from sewage. Utilizing a rotating biological contactor (RBC), this study investigated the long-term efficacy of treating real, human urine with PN/A over a wide range of urine concentrations, ranging from very dilute (0.6 g N L-1) to undiluted urine (5 g N L-1). At urine concentrations expected in source-separated toilets (2 g N L-1), the RBC reactor achieved a nitrogen removal efficiency of 94% ± 6 at a removal rate of 0.3 ± 0.1 g N L-1 d-1. On undiluted urine (5 g N L-1), the reactor maintained this rate and even reached superior nitrogen removal efficiency (97 ± 3%). Keeping a total ammoniacal nitrogen residual of 25-100 mg N L-1 at low dissolved oxygen (0.5 g O2 L-1) enabled the growth of excess anoxic ammonium oxidizing bacteria (AnAOB) capacity, yielding resilient and robust long-term operation. Batch activity tests showed no significant change in maximum AnAOB nitrogen removal rate with increasing urine strength. The PN/A RBC had an average N2O emission factor of 1.32 ± 0.30 % g N-N2O g N-1 loaded, resulting in a similar carbon footprint compared to conventional nitrification/denitrification (6 kg CO2-eq kg N-1 loaded). The PN/A RBC therefore provides an effective, simple and resource-lean solution for low-maintenance human urine treatment in a building-level decentralized setting, and its wide implementation will result in a significant reduction of nitrogen load reaching the existing sewage treatment plants, extending their treatment capacity.","center":"University of Antwerp","data_type":"Other","submission_date":"2026/07/11 00:00","sample_cols":["accession","title","tier","context","collection_date","collection_year","country","region","place_id","place","locati